<p>
  Bloating that builds through the day is one of the most common digestive complaints — and one of the most
  misdiagnosed. It is treated as a food problem, a water problem, a posture problem. Most often,
  <strong>it is a digestion problem</strong>: food is spending too long in a gut that is not moving or breaking
  it down efficiently.
</p>

<h2>Why your gut is fine in the morning and not by dinner</h2>
<p>
  Think of your digestive tract as a conveyor belt. It is supposed to clear each meal before the next one
  arrives. When digestion is sluggish, each meal adds load to a belt that is already behind — gas and
  distension accumulate, and by late afternoon the total is highest. That is why the pattern is so predictable:
  flat stomach in the morning, swollen by evening.
</p>
<p>
  Common drivers include:
</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Low stomach acid or enzyme output</strong> — food arrives in the gut less broken down than it should be</li>
  <li><strong>Imbalanced gut microbes</strong> — the wrong bacteria fermenting food into gas</li>
  <li><strong>Inflammation of the gut lining</strong> — the gut reacts to foods it would normally handle easily</li>
  <li><strong>Meal pacing</strong> — eating on a schedule your digestion cannot keep up with</li>
</ul>

<h2>Why probiotics alone rarely fix it</h2>
<p>
  The supplement aisle treats all bloating as a bacteria shortage. But if digestion is slow and the lining is
  inflamed, adding bacteria to a struggling gut is like restocking a store with the shelves collapsed.
  <strong>Order matters:</strong> restore digestion first, clear what does not belong, repair the lining — and
  only then repopulate.
</p>

<h2>What actually works: the 90-day sequence</h2>
<p>
  EmpowerX3's 90-Day Gut Rebuild follows exactly that order, in three phases:
</p>
<ol>
  <li><strong>Days 1–30 · Remove & Defend</strong> — restore digestion, clear out what does not belong and calm the gut, building up gradually with coaching if detox symptoms show up</li>
